    },
    {
      "block_id": "page-0847-block-0035",
      "order": 35,
      "text": "Psychotropics with a risk of weight gain after bariatric surgery\n",
      "bbox": [
        61.88999938964844,
        351.7289733886719,
        355.8400573730469,
        363.5789794921875
      ],
      "text_sha256": "a8b58545ac4b1756e73b5e22cdbfe658439a3c80787f437a0117fe1d4b8469e6"
    },
    {
      "block_id": "page-0847-block-0036",
      "order": 36,
      "text": "It is estimated that 10–20% of patients regain a significant amount of weight after bari-\natric surgery.49 There is no information on how psychotropics associated with weight \ngain affect outcomes after surgery, but high-risk drugs should probably be avoided. \nPatients’ individual clinical circumstances should be considered (especially if stable on \ntreatment and at a high risk of relapse) as there is evidence that uncontrolled mental \nillness is a risk factor for weight regain.49\n",
      "bbox": [
        61.88999938964844,
        369.63897705078125,
        436.714111328125,
        441.3589782714844
      ],
      "text_sha256": "485f7fe982de2756099bee43178cf1b0712a5adf31cb264369335e2ff467e022"
    },
    {
      "block_id": "page-0847-block-0037",
      "order": 37,
      "text": "Alcohol50,51\n",
      "bbox": [
        61.88999938964844,
        458.7939758300781,
        117.03972625732422,
        471.8289794921875
      ],
      "text_sha256": "ff2cff17d7cfaeacd00304de940cf8f324258d0d37db8fa6ba6526fe5c7dc332"
    },
    {
      "block_id": "page-0847-block-0038",
      "order": 38,
      "text": "Gastric bypass surgery is associated with accelerated alcohol absorption, higher maxi-\nmum alcohol concentrations and a longer time to elimination. There is also an increased \nrisk of alcohol misuse disorders after gastric bypass. Data are less clear for sleeve gas-\ntrectomy and there is no evidence that gastric banding leads to any changes.\n",
      "bbox": [
        61.88999938964844,
        477.63897705078125,
        436.7199401855469,
        525.3590087890625
      ],
      "text_sha256": "1ad9f30a1b4764b2f4b05517f933b22cf81d8232ef9deb6561f21af8d0372055"
    },
    {
      "block_id": "page-0847-block-0039",
      "order": 39,
      "text": "Wider considerations52\n",
      "bbox": [
        61.88999938964844,
        542.7940063476562,
        178.41505432128906,
        555.8289794921875
      ],
      "text_sha256": "45a465dc9db8e8f95f675b751e197f3c3438675dabc318c7208976524dde69fc"
    },
    {
      "block_id": "page-0847-block-0040",
      "order": 40,
      "text": "In practice, many patients may not require significant changes to drug treatment after \nsurgery. Relapse of symptoms after surgery may not be related to altered drug pharma-\ncokinetics. Although improvements in mental health are to be anticipated, deterioration \ncan also occur due to a range of factors, including unmet weight-loss expectations, poor \ntolerability and dissatisfaction after surgical treatment.\n",
      "bbox": [
        61.88999938964844,
        561.6389770507812,
        436.7099914550781,
        621.3590087890625
      ],
      "text_sha256": "92a90b351106206085c1dccbaa69acbd70c9bf339856fbd2f42bd9cbce350489"
    }
